1 NAME Dr. Jean Pierre Bondurant
1 NAME Jean-Pierre /Bondurant/
2 GIVN Jean-Pierre
2 SURN Bondurant
2 _MARNM Bondurant
1 NAME Dr. Jean Pierre Bondurant
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Bondurant Family Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Prominent planter and large land owner
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 JUL 1677
2 ADDR
3 CITY Génolhac
3 STAE Occitanie
3 CTRY France
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 SEP 1734
2 ADDR
3 CITY Manakin
3 STAE Province of Virginia
1 OCCU apothecary, landowner, Doctor
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} JEAN PIERRE BONDURANT dit [called] Cougousset – b. 18 Sept. 1677 in Genolhac. He was first baptized in the Huguenot Temple and then rebaptized a Catholic 13 Feb. 1684. He left Genolhac at age 20; arrived at his Unc
2 CONC le Guillaume Barjon’s church in Aarau Switzerland and professed his sin of conversion and renounced Catholicism 3 Oct. 1697. He moved on to Germany then to England to be part of the 2nd ship of Huguenot refugees sent to Virginia. 
2 CONC He married after he’d been in Virginia a time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: http://www.behindthename.com/name/franc1… http://www.worldfamilies.net/forum/index…
2 CONT =<nowiki>-------------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT from http://lawrencefamhis.com/bondurant-o/p12.htm#i299 and http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~lawrpaul/bondurant-o/p41.htm#i299 :
2 CONT '''Jean-Pierre Bondurant''' was born on 18 July 1677 in Génolhac, Gard, France. ( 1 ) Father	Jean Pierre Bondurant b. c 1636, d. 24 May 1694 Mother	Gabrielle Barjon b. 18 Jan 1643, d. 23 Mar 1695 He married '''Ann Tanner''', daugh
2 CONC ter of Edward Tanner [Sr.] and Mary Hatcher, circa 1708 in Manakin Town, Virginia. (1,2 )
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Pierre died before 25 January 1734/35 in Manakin Town, Virginia. (1,3,2) (his will 25/1/1734) Jean was also known as John Peter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Pierre was first baptised a Huguenot, then at the age of seven rebaptised a Catholic in order to preserve his right to inherit family property. With the Revocation both of his parents, and his grandfather and step-grandmother
2 CONC  renounced their Huguenot faith and were "newly converted" Catholics.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was orphaned and left in the guardianship of his cousin, Andre Bondurant who was an apothecary and also the mayor of Génolhac. In September 1697, with Andre's consent, Jean Pierre's property, the mills at l'Aribal and Calquière
2 CONC s inherited from his parents, were sold. Jean Pierre left France and arrived in Aarau, Switzerland in February 1697/98 where he joined his maternal uncle, Guillaume Barjon, Pastor of the Huguenot refugee Church there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 3, 1697, Jean Pierre recanted the Catholic faith and became a Huguenot again. The following is recorded in the congregational record:
2 CONT
2 CONT >The 3rd of October 1697, Seigneur Jean Pierre Bondurant, apothecary, presented himself in front of us, claiming that he was extremely affected by the fault he committed in his youth, which was to attend the worship of the Roman [
2 CONC Catholic] Church; showing his repentance by asking God to forgive this sin and after he claimed that he would live and die in our Holy Religion, he was admitted into the peace of the Church and to participate in the Holy Sacrament
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT >He signed this present deed along with us: Henry Malbois and Pierre Brochet, both hat makers who took refuge in the town of Aarau, and undersigned by myself:
2 CONT
2 CONT >[signed by]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Barjon
2 CONT >refugee Pastor
2 CONT >JP Bondurant
2 CONT >Henry Malbois
2 CONT >Brochet4
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Jean Pierre had left France with money from the sale of his mills, he did not appear on the Swiss charity records as did the Barjons and most refugee Huguenots. Being financially independent also shielded him from scrutiny
2 CONC  and perhaps enabled him to buy his first land when he later went to Virginia.4
2 CONT
2 CONT He was listed as a "fugitive from the Kingdom because of religion" in 1712. This late date is not significant, as such listings were normallly late; and his father's cousin was Consul General in charge of the listings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1699, in the company of Pastor Barjon and other refugees, he went to Karlshafen, Germany. There is no record of his travel from Karlshafen to England, but he was among Huguenot refugees arriving at the mouth of the James River 
2 CONC in Virginia on 20 September 1700 aboard the ship Ye Peter and Anthony which had sailed from England. This was the "second transport" of Huguenots from London.
2 CONT
2 CONT The immigrants were taken up the James River in smaller boats as far as the shoals (present day Richmond.) They went overland to Manakin Town, where they joined the "first transport" settlers who had occupied a deserted Monacan In
2 CONC dian village near Fine Creek the previous year. Jean stayed with the colony until 1701 and then, as a single man, went elsewhere looking for more opportunity. He practiced medicine in Henrico County (part of which later became Goo
2 CONC chland County) for many years.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre's grandfather was a Doctor of Law and was apparently successful. His father, Jean Pierre Bondurant, Sieur de Cougoussat, Advocate, did not seem to be as successful as he was in debt when he died. At one time, Jean Pier
2 CONC re, the immigrant, was forced to accept public assistance from the Church of Rome. He was apprenticed to his father's cousin, Andre, a Master Apothecary, where he learned enough medicine to be accounted a Doctor in the Colonies in
2 CONC  Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1704, the Huguenot men petitioned to become citizens of the colony and by an act passed in 1705, they, including Jean Pierre, were granted citizenship by the Governor and House of Burgesses.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Pierre obtained 200 acres of land on Old Town Creek, near present Matoaca, across the Appomattox River from Petersburg. He sold that on 29 Dec 1708 to John Wilson Sr., acknowledged in court 1 June 1709. In 1711 he registered 
2 CONC a cattle mark with the Virginia governor. On March 24 he purchased, from the King of England, 400 acres on the south side of the James River located on Jones Creek and Matthews Branch in Henrico County, later part of Goochland Cou
2 CONC nty. In 1729, sons Peter and John were listed as tithables on this land, but the father does not appear on the tithables lists until 1730. This seems to indicate that the parents were living elsewhere and the sons had come ahead t
2 CONC o prepare the new land. This land was divided among his sons in his will in 1734. Jean Peire was elected to the Vestry of King William Parish Church, but died before he could take office.4
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known if he was married more than once and, if so, which children belong to each wife. A wife Ann, the mother of his son Peter, is mentioned in his will. Many researchers had thought that his wife was Ann Faure but this 
2 CONC appears incorrect. She may have been Ann Tanner. The will of Mrs. Mary Tanner, Albermarle Co., Virginia, in the 1760s, left $1 to her daughter Ann Bondurant. Whether this is the widow of Jean Pierre has not been proven. Also, the 
2 CONC marriage date cannot be confirmed. The date shown is given by some researchers but it has never been proven.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1990 the Bondurant Family Association erected a fence and placed a commemorative plaque at the posited graves of Jean Pierre and Ann Bondurant. The graveyard is located on Birdsong Lane (Road No. 1217) near Powhatan, in Powhata
2 CONC n County, Virginia. The plaque reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT To the glory of God and in memory of Jean Pierre Bondurant and his wife Ann. Born in Génolhac, France 18 July 1677, Jean Pierre (John Peter) Bondurant escaped to Switzerland in 1697, and reached Jamestown with other Huguenots in 1
2 CONC 700. Trained as an apothecary, he practiced medicine in Virginia where he married Ann. Members of King William Parish. He died near Manakin 1734/35. Their five children were John, Peter, Joseph, Ann and Frances. Erected 1990 by de
2 CONC scendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean made a will on 25 September 1734. '''The will of John Peter Bondurant, Gouchland County, Virginia:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen, I John Peter Bondurant, being sick and weak by of good sound disposition mind and memory, all praise be given to God for it, and now minding to settle my worldly estate which it has please God to bestow up
2 CONC on me before I depart this life: I do make and appoint this my last will and testament in manner and form following:
2 CONT
2 CONT First: I give my soul to God who gave it, and my body to the earth from which it was taken, to be decently buried according to the discretion of my Executors hereafter named.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give and bequeth unto my son John Bundurant, one hundred and forty acres of land which he now dwelleth on and one horse and one hog and blanket and rug and sheet and hide and bed cord and frou pot, and dish and basin and t
2 CONC hree plates and one cow and calf that he is now possessed of him and to his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item:I give and bequeath unto my son Peter Bondurant one hundred and fifty acres of land on Age Creek and up along creek Goos, and a horse named Smoker and one cow and calf and two sows and four shoats and one pewter dish and one 
2 CONC basin and one porringer and six pewter spoons and one frou pot and one rug and blanket and sheet and brown lining to make him a bed, to him and his heirs lawfully begotten forever, but if one or all of my sons die without issue, t
2 CONC hen to the survivor and his heirs lawfully begotten forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give and bequeath to my son Joseph Bondurant one hundred and fifty acres of land with the purtenances belonging thereto, where he now dwelleth, and one cow and calf in the possession of James Ford and two sows and four sho
2 CONC ats and one ram sheep, and one mair with the hors colts the she has and the mair fols to return to me and two dishes, one large and one small, one basin and six plates and one Porringer and one paint pot and one sow and four shoat
2 CONC s, to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give and bequeath to my daughter Ann Ford one heifer eighteen months old, and one sow and pigs to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give to my daughter Frances Salle one heifer at the age of eighteen months and one sow and pigs to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I do give unto my son Peter Bondurant all my wearing clothes and it is my desire that my wife keep them and let him have them as she sees fit and my carpenter tools and shoemakers tools and croscut saw and wagons I lend for th
2 CONC e use of my wife and two sons and their heirs be no hinderance or molestation of any persons or persons whatsoever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item: I give to my loving wife Ann Bondurant after all my just debts and funeral charges and legacies are paid, all the remainder of my estate real and personal in this place or also wherever to her and her heirs forever, and I do
2 CONC  allow my loving wife Ann Bondurant whole and sole Executor of this my last will and testament, revoking all other wills by me made heretofore.
2 CONT
2 CONT As witness my hand and fixt my seal, this 25th day of September 1734. 
2 CONT
2 CONT J. P. Bondurant (Seal)
2 CONT
2 CONT Test: John Cook, James Ford, John Bondurant
2 CONT
2 CONT It is my desire that my son Peter Bondurant heirs his share of land on the S. & E. of Age Branch and my son John Bondurant and my son Joseph Bondurant to have their share on the S. & W. of my branch to be equally divided between t
2 CONC hem according as the lines go and to have the liberty of cutting any pine or pine trees upon my son Peter Bondurant's land for their house.3
2 CONT
2 CONT His will was proved on 25 January 1734/35. At the court held for Goochland County January 25th, 1734, this will was proved by the oaths of John Cook and James Ford, two of the witnesses hereto and was admitted to probate.
2 CONT
2 CONT H. Wood, County Court Clerk.3
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Tanner b. circa 1695
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ann Bondurant b. 1710, d. a 8 Mar 1758
2 CONT * John Peter Bondurant [Sr.] b. c 1710, d. c 1774
2 CONT * Peter Jacob Bondurant b. c 1711, d. c 1735
2 CONT * Frances Jane Bondurant b. c 1712, d. c 1777
2 CONT * Joseph Augustine Bondurant b. c 1720, d. 11 Aug 1806
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [S4] The Huguenot Society. The Huguenot 1981-1983. Midlothian, Virginia: The Huguenot Society, the Founders of Manakin in the Colony of Virginia, Inc.).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. [S511] The Bondurant Family, compiled by Wiley B. Grinnell, Sr.. 1979. Private Printing, Copy in researcher's file, Fairfield Glade, Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. [S116] The Huguenot Society. The Huguenot 1979-1981. Midlothian, Virginia: The Huguenot Society, Founders of Manakin in the Colony of Virginia, Inc., 1984).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. [S476] Warren, Mary Bondurant. The Bondurants of Génolhac, France. Athens, Georgia 30606: Heritage Papers, 2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jean Pierre Bondurant was born 18 July 1678 at Genolhac, Gard, Languedoc-Rossillon, France the son of Dr. Joseph Augustine and Elizabeth Ann Chastain Bondurant. He married 1708 in Manakin Town, Rhonda Ann Faure the daughter of Dan
2 CONC iel Issac and Mary Ann Chastain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Known Children: Jean Pierre Bondurant, II 1709-  Eliizabeth Anne Bondurant 1714-1814 Md Captain Jaques Faure Died Buckingham County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
