1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Update 28th July 2025: W.J.E.W.
2 CONT Jean Honoré Robert de Paul de Lamanon, known as Robert de Lamanon was a French botanist, physicist, geologist and meteorologist. Jean Honoré Robert de Paul de Lamanon, known as Robert de Lamanon (6 December 1752, Salon-de-Provence
2 CONC  – 11 December 1787, Aʻasu, Tutuila, Samoan Islands) was a French botanist, physicist, geologist and meteorologist. He joined several scientific expeditions and eventually died on one (the La Perouse expedition).[1] 
2 CONT He belonged to the lordly family of Lamanon, ennobled in 1572. His father was Jean François de Paul de Lamanon and Anne de Baldony (born in Aix-en-Provence). He had two sisters and an elder brother, Auguste Paul de Lamanon (1748–1
2 CONC 820). Auguste was destined for the French Navy by his father, becoming a garde de la marine at Toulon, then an officer. 
2 CONT Robert's brother Auguste left the Navy on his father's death to devote himself to the study of sciences and letters. Robert left holy orders and together they set out on a trip round Europe and the French provinces collecting plan
2 CONC ts, documents, manuscripts and objects d'art. They perfected their studies in Paris and there met the most famous scholars of the era : Laplace, Jussieu, d'Alembert, the Monge brothers, Volney, Malesherbes, and Condorcet, secretar
2 CONC y of the Académie des sciences.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert's paper on fossils found in the gypsum formation around Paris, Observations sur la Physique, contributed to practice of turning physical geography into geohistory. The practice was still novel at the time, Nicolas Desmarest
2 CONC  (1725-1815) publishing his essay on recognizing prismatic basalt as old lava streams only three years before[1] in 1774.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reported to be "energetic and full of lively curiosity," on a trip from Paris to England, Robert ordered himself to be tied to the mainmast during a violent storm so that he could contemplate the spectacle.[2][3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial to La Perouse massacred expedition members was built in 1883 in Aʻasu village on Massacre Bay on the Northwest coast of the island of Tutuila (then — the island of Maouna), Samoan Islands. The monument surmounted by a c
2 CONC ross bears the words "Morts pour la Science et la Patrie" ("Those who died for Science and Fatherland") and names of eleven French sailors who were killed in the encounter with the Samoans, among them the name of Robert de Lamanon
2 CONC .[4]
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT * Voir [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_de_Lamanon '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT * A memorial for those that died at Tutuila was erected in 1883. In reads "Morts pour la Science et la Patrie''" - For those that died for science and fatherland
