1 OCCU Marie Louise Henriette Jeanne, *15.8.1725, +guillotined 1793; m.19.2.1743 Jules Hercule Meriadec de Rohan, Pr de Guemene, Duc de Montbazon (*25.3.1726, +10.12.1800); she also had issue by Charles Edward Stuart
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie_Louise_de_La_Tour_d%27Auvergne
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Louise de La Tour d'Auvergne (Marie Louise Henriette Jeanne; 15 August 1725 – 1793) was a French noblewoman and member of the House of La Tour d'Auvergne. She was the Princess of Guéméné by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Louise was the first child born to Charles Godefroy de La Tour d'Auvergne and his wife Maria Karolina Sobieska, grand daughter of John III Sobieski and an older sister of Clementina Sobieski, wife of James Francis Edward Stu
2 CONC art. She had a younger brother Godefroy Charles Henri who succeeded their father as Duke of Bouillon.
2 CONT
2 CONT A great-granddaughter of the John III Sobieski, she was also an exceptionally wealthy heiress. Because of the latter, Louis XV of France took a great personal interest in her marriage prospects.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mademoiselle d'Auveregne was a proposed bride for the Honoré III, Prince of Monaco. He was the son of the late Louise Hippolyte, Princess of Monaco and her consort Jacques Goyon de Matignon. Even though marriage plans were announc
2 CONC ed to the court on 26 January 1741, the end the marriage never materialised.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, she married Jules Hercule Mériadec de Rohan, Duke of Montbazon, Prince of Guéméné.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple were married in Paris on 19 February 1743 when she was not yet seventeen. He was the son of Hercule Mériadec de Rohan, Duke of Montbazon, Prince of Guéméné (1688–1757) and Louise Gabrielle Julie de Rohan[1] (1704–1741).
2 CONC  Two years later a son was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her mother, Marie Louise could count a Holy Roman Empress and Electress of Saxony as cousins. Her aunt, Anne Marie Louise de La Tour d'Auvergne had already married into the House of Rohan, who ranked as Foreign princes at 
2 CONC the court of Versailles. As such, this gave them the style of Highness and the right to out rank certain other member of the court.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1746 Marie Louise contracted smallpox which, in those days, was often a fatal disease.
2 CONT
2 CONT During her convalescence, her family received a sympathy note from her first cousin, Charles Edward Stuart, also known as the Young Pretender. Having recovered, in August 1747, the couple met and Marie Louise passionately fell in 
2 CONC love with this first cousin, who returned her feelings, and they began an affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Louise's circles adultery was widely accepted so long as it was done discreetly. However, as neither Marie Louise nor her husband had been unfaithful before, her mother-in-law kept a very strict eye on her as her husband was wi
2 CONC th the French army in Holland and her servants had been ordered, by her mother-in-law, to guard her virtue. Marie Louise and Charles then resorted to secret midnight coach-rides; however, her mother-in-law had alerted the Paris po
2 CONC lice who reported what went on.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a while, Marie Louise found herself pregnant and resorted to sleeping with her returned husband to make him believe he was the father, but this enraged the jealous Charles into creating a scene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet Marie Louise's husband did nothing and gossip began to spread. In January 1748, confronted by her own father as well as her mother-in-law, Marie Louise was forced to write to Charles ending their affaire. Nevertheless, Charles
2 CONC  was still allowed to visit her and her family in order to refute the gossip about their affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT In despair, Marie Louise wrote more letters to Charles, threatening suicide if he didn't come to see her. He did so some three months later, again in a midnight assignation, but only to tell her he had a new mistress, Clementina W
2 CONC alkinshaw. Clementina later gave birth to Charlotte Stuart, Duchess of Albany,[1] the only one of Charles' children to survive infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 July 1748, Marie Louise gave birth to a son, who was baptised Charles Godefroi Sophie Jules Marie de Rohan. It was her mother-in-law who wrote to Charles's father, 'Old Pretender', in Rome to tell of the news, but not that t
2 CONC his child was his grandchild. Despite having been accepted as a member of the Rohan family, several genealogical books note the fact that the Rohans fail to mention this child again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise lived at least another thirty-three years and apparently was never unfaithful again. To all appearances she was a good wife and mother to her first-born but never had another child. She made occasional appearances at court,
2 CONC  then later in life became religious and devoted much of her time to charity.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she died she, too, was buried in the convent des Feuillants together with her second child. However, there is uncertainty as to exactly when she died: either naturally in September 1781, or on the guillotine in 1793. The latt
2 CONC er is widely accepted.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is through Marie Louise that the present Princes of Guéméné are pretenders to the Duchy of Bouillon.
