1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Updated 8th July 2023 by W.J.E.W. with photo: - Not to confuse with "Francis Liardet" listed as son of W.F.E Liardet.This Captain Francis Liardet is Correctly Profiled as son of Major John Robert James William Tell
2 CONC  Liardet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Francis Liardet - Born June 14th 1798 Chelsea [location unknown] Died March 1st 1863, age about 65 in Royal Hospital Greenwich
2 CONT 2nd Son of John Robert James William Tell Liardet & Félicité Perpétue Catherine (de Paul de Lamanon d'Albe)Liardet.
2 CONT Brother of William Liardet [half], Louisa Felicia Mary (Liardet) Moffitt and Caroline Frederica Liardet - Spouse Unknown [children unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Liardet, Francis, Born Nov 15 1839 in Melbourne, Victoria, Australia, Died 1854 to Wilbraham Frederick Evelyn Liardet
2 CONT '''
2 CONT GOOGLE COPIED: Notes on Officer: Biography ref:1059
2 CONT LIARDET, FRANCIS (1798–1863), captain in the navy, second son of John Liardet by the Lady Perpetue Catherine de Paul de Lamanon d'Albe, was born at Chelsea on June 14th 1798. He entered the navy in 1809, on board the Mercury friga
2 CONC te, with Captain the Hon. Henry Duncan, in the Mediterranean. In March 1810 he was transferred to the Belvidera frigate, with Captain Richard Byron, on the coast of Africa, and afterwards on the North American station, and was sli
2 CONC ghtly wounded in her running fight with and escape from the United States' squadron under Commodore Rodgers on June 23rd 1812 (James, Naval Hist.1860, v. 357; Roosevelt, Naval War of 1812, p. 74). After an active commission the Be
2 CONC lvidera was paid off in October 1814, and for the next two years he served in the West Indies on board the Warrior and Forester sloop. After the peace he devoted himself for some time to the study of mathematics and navigation, an
2 CONC d in 1819 went a voyage to the East Indies as mate of a merchant ship. In May 1821, Liardet was appointed to the Hyperion of 42 guns, going out to the Cape of Good Hope, and afterwards to the West Indies, where he was moved into t
2 CONC he Union schooner, employed in the suppression of piracy, in which service he was severely wounded, July 25th 1823. On March 18th 1824, he was promoted to the rank of lieutenant, and appointed to command the Lion schooner employed
2 CONC  in the same service. In her he destroyed several nests of pirates on the coast of Cuba, captured nine of their vessels, some of their prizes, and a slaver. He was first lieutenant of the Procris, attending on the Duke of Clarence
2 CONC , then lord high admiral, in 1827–8, and of the Jaseur at the Cape of Good Hope from 1828 to 1832, in which period he was three times officially reported as having saved life by jumping overboard, once in a sea abounding in sharks
2 CONC  (Young, Acts of Gallantry, 1872, pp. 22, 35). From 1833 to 1835 he was first lieutenant of the Snake on the South American station, and from 1835 to 1838 of the Cleopatra frigate with the Hon. Charles Grey. On June 28th 1838, he 
2 CONC was promoted to be commander, and in the following January was appointed to the Powerful of 84 guns, carrying the broad pennant of Commodore (afterwards Admiral Sir Charles) Napier [q. v.], as second in command in the Mediterranea
2 CONC n, on the coast of Syria, and especially at the bombardment of St. Jean d'Acre. For his services during this time, when he was frequently in actual command of the Powerful, the commodore being employed on shore, Liardet was promot
2 CONC ed to post rank 4 Nov. 1840.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following year he accepted an appointment as agent for the New Zealand Company at Taranaki. He arrived there towards the end of September. On 29 Nov., in expectation of an attack by the Maoris, he was endeavoring to clear t
2 CONC he vent of a rusty old four-pounder when an untimely explosion of the charge destroyed the sight of one eye and seriously injured the other. For several years he was almost totally blind. In February 1842 he left Taranaki for Sydn
2 CONC ey, whence he returned to England. During his enforced retirement he wrote or dictated ‘Professional Recollections on points of Seamanship, Discipline, & c.,’ 8vo, 1849, and the ‘Midshipman's Companion,’ 12mo, 1851. In January 185
2 CONC 6 he was appointed one of the captains of Greenwich Hospital, and he published ‘Friendly Hints to the Young Naval Lieutenant,’ 12mo, 1858. He died in the Hospital on 1 March 1863, and was buried in the mausoleum of the old cemeter
2 CONC y. A marble bust by T. Milnes is in the Painted Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1842 Liardet married Caroline Anne, sister of Sir Edmund Filmer, bart., and widow of Lieutenant John Jervis Gregory, R.N., and had two daughters and a son.
